Most beans are registered through annotations, but sometimes you need to register beans programmatically — for dynamic configurations, plugin systems, or registering beans from third-party libraries that you can't annotate.

```java
@Component
public class DynamicBeanRegistrar implements BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor {

    @Override
    public void postProcessBeanDefinitionRegistry(BeanDefinitionRegistry registry) {
        GenericBeanDefinition definition = new GenericBeanDefinition();
        definition.setBeanClass(DynamicService.class);
        definition.setScope(BeanDefinition.SCOPE_SINGLETON);
        definition.setLazyInit(false);
        definition.getPropertyValues().add("timeout", 5000);
        registry.registerBeanDefinition("dynamicService", definition);
    }

    @Override
    public void postProcessBeanFactory(ConfigurableListableBeanFactory factory) {
        // Can modify existing definitions here
        BeanDefinition existing = factory.getBeanDefinition("someBean");
        existing.setScope("prototype");
    }
}
```

`BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor` runs before `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` — it can register new definitions that other post-processors will later process. The `postProcessBeanFactory()` method can modify existing definitions: change a bean's scope, mark it as lazy, or alter its constructor arguments.

This is the same mechanism Spring uses internally. `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor` (which processes `@Configuration` and `@ComponentScan`) is itself a `BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor`. When it scans your packages and finds `@Service` classes, it creates `BeanDefinition` objects for each one and registers them — exactly like the code above, but driven by annotations instead of manual API calls.

To inspect registered bean definitions during debugging, inject `ConfigurableApplicationContext` and call `getBeanDefinitionNames()` to list every definition, or `getBeanDefinition(name)` to inspect a specific one. This is faster than guessing why a bean exists or doesn't exist.

Custom events are plain Java objects — no base class required since Spring 4.2. Define a record or class with the relevant data, publish it with `ApplicationEventPublisher`, and listen with `@EventListener`.

```java
public record PaymentCompletedEvent(String orderId, String paymentId, BigDecimal amount) {}

@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    @Transactional
    public void completePayment(Payment payment) {
        paymentRepository.save(payment);
        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCompletedEvent(
            payment.getOrderId(), payment.getId(), payment.getAmount()));
    }
}
```

The event is published inside a `@Transactional` method. If a synchronous `@EventListener` sends a confirmation email and the email service throws, the transaction rolls back — the payment is not saved, but the user might have already seen a "processing" message.

`@TransactionalEventListener` solves this by deferring execution until the transaction outcome is known:

```java
@Component
public class PaymentNotificationListener {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onPaymentCompleted(PaymentCompletedEvent event) {
        emailService.sendReceipt(event.orderId(), event.amount());
    }

    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_ROLLBACK)
    public void onPaymentFailed(PaymentCompletedEvent event) {
        alertService.notifyPaymentRollback(event.paymentId());
    }
}
```

The `AFTER_COMMIT` listener only fires if the transaction succeeds — the payment is guaranteed to be persisted. The `AFTER_ROLLBACK` listener fires only on failure. This separation prevents the classic "email sent but order wasn't saved" bug that plagues applications with synchronous event handling.

The pattern is simple: inject `ResourceLoader`, resolve the path with a prefix, and read the stream. This works identically whether the application runs from an IDE, an exploded directory, or a fat JAR.

```java
@Service
public class TemplateService {
    private final ResourceLoader resourceLoader;

    public TemplateService(ResourceLoader resourceLoader) {
        this.resourceLoader = resourceLoader;
    }

    public String loadTemplate(String name) throws IOException {
        Resource resource = resourceLoader.getResource("classpath:templates/" + name);
        if (!resource.exists()) {
            throw new TemplateNotFoundException(name);
        }
        try (InputStream is = resource.getInputStream()) {
            return new String(is.readAllBytes(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        }
    }
}
```

The `exists()` check prevents a confusing `FileNotFoundException`. The `try-with-resources` ensures the stream is closed even if reading fails. Using `getInputStream()` instead of `getFile()` is critical — `getFile()` throws `FileNotFoundException` for resources inside JARs because there's no physical file to reference.

For loading multiple resources matching a pattern, use `ResourcePatternResolver` (which `ApplicationContext` also implements):

```java
Resource[] templates = ((ResourcePatternResolver) resourceLoader)
    .getResources("classpath:templates/*.html");
```

The `classpath*:` prefix (note the asterisk) scans all JARs on the classpath, not just the current module. Use it when templates might come from dependency JARs. Without the asterisk, only the first matching classpath entry is searched — resources in other JARs are invisible.

The most common use of profiles is swapping implementations between environments — an in-memory cache for development, Redis for production; an H2 database for testing, PostgreSQL for production.

```java
@Configuration
public class CacheConfig {
    @Bean
    @Profile("dev")
    public CacheManager devCacheManager() {
        return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager("products", "users");
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("prod")
    public CacheManager prodCacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory factory) {
        return RedisCacheManager.builder(factory)
            .cacheDefaults(RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(30)))
            .build();
    }
}
```

Activate a profile with `spring.profiles.active=prod` in any property source — environment variable, system property, or `application.properties`. Multiple profiles can be active simultaneously: `spring.profiles.active=prod,metrics` activates both.

For programmatic access to the environment, inject `Environment` directly. This is useful for conditional logic that doesn't fit the `@Profile` annotation model — checking whether a specific property is set, reading the active profiles, or resolving properties with default values.

```java
@Service
public class FeatureToggleService {
    private final Environment env;

    public boolean isEnabled(String feature) {
        return env.getProperty("feature." + feature + ".enabled",
            Boolean.class, false);
    }

    public boolean isProduction() {
        return Arrays.asList(env.getActiveProfiles()).contains("prod");
    }
}
```

The `getProperty()` overload with a default value is safer than `@Value` for optional properties — it returns the default without throwing, and you can call it at any time, not just during bean creation. For most property access, `@Value` or `@ConfigurationProperties` is more idiomatic. Reserve programmatic `Environment` access for dynamic lookups where the property key isn't known at compile time.

Creating a composed annotation is straightforward: define a new annotation and annotate it with the annotations you want to compose. Spring will detect the composed annotations through its annotation scanning mechanism.

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Service
@Transactional
@Validated
public @interface BusinessService {
    @AliasFor(annotation = Service.class, attribute = "value")
    String value() default "";
}
```

The `@AliasFor` annotation is the key to making composed annotations configurable. It bridges an attribute on your annotation to an attribute on a composed annotation. Here, `@BusinessService("paymentService")` passes the value to `@Service("paymentService")` — the bean gets the specified name.

Usage is simple and eliminates repetition:

```java
@BusinessService
public class PaymentService {
    // Automatically @Service + @Transactional + @Validated
    public void processPayment(@Valid PaymentRequest request) {
        // Validation runs (@Validated), transaction wraps the method (@Transactional)
    }
}
```

For annotations with attributes you want to expose, `@AliasFor` creates the mapping:

```java
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Cacheable
public @interface CachedForMinutes {
    @AliasFor(annotation = Cacheable.class, attribute = "value")
    String[] cacheNames();

    @AliasFor(annotation = Cacheable.class, attribute = "key")
    String key() default "";
}
```

This lets you write `@CachedForMinutes(cacheNames = "products")` instead of `@Cacheable(value = "products")`. The composed annotation provides a more domain-specific name while the underlying behavior comes from `@Cacheable`. One caution: over-composing annotations obscures what's actually happening. A `@BusinessService` that bundles five annotations is convenient until someone needs to debug why validation isn't firing and can't find a `@Validated` annotation on the class.

To understand the IoC container's behavior in your application, you can trace which beans are created and in what order. This is invaluable for debugging startup performance issues and unexpected initialization sequences.

```java
@Component
public class BeanCreationTracer implements BeanPostProcessor, PriorityOrdered {

    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        if (!beanName.startsWith("org.springframework")) {
            log.debug("Creating bean: {} [{}]", beanName,
                bean.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
        return bean;
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        return Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE;
    }
}
```

`PriorityOrdered` with `HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE` ensures this tracer runs before other post-processors, so it sees bean creation at the earliest possible point. Filtering out `org.springframework` beans reduces noise — Spring creates hundreds of internal beans that aren't relevant to most debugging sessions.

The container's dependency resolution is deterministic: given the same definitions and dependencies, the creation order is the same every startup. But the order is determined by the dependency graph, not by alphabetical order or file order. Adding a new dependency to a bean can change the creation order of seemingly unrelated beans because it changes the graph traversal path.

For startup performance analysis, Spring Boot Actuator's `/actuator/startup` endpoint (Spring Boot 2.4+) provides detailed timing for each bean creation phase — how long the constructor took, how long post-processing took, and how long initialization callbacks took. This is more useful than the tracer for identifying which beans are slowing down startup.

**Phase 4 — Context preparation:** The main application class (your `@SpringBootApplication` class) is registered as a bean definition. Environment is set on the context. `ApplicationContextInitializer` instances are applied. Banner is printed.

**Phase 5 — Context refresh:** This is `AbstractApplicationContext.refresh()` — the massive method that does everything. Bean definitions are loaded (component scanning, `@Configuration` processing, auto-configuration discovery). `BeanFactoryPostProcessors` run (placeholder resolution, configuration class processing). `BeanPostProcessors` are registered. All singleton beans are instantiated. The embedded web server (Tomcat, Jetty, Netty) is created and started.

If startup fails, it fails in this phase. `BeanCreationException`, `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException`, `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException` — all originate from `refresh()`. The exception chain tells you which bean failed, and tracing the chain reveals the root cause.

**Phase 6 — Runner execution:** After refresh completes successfully, Spring Boot calls `CommandLineRunner.run()` and `ApplicationRunner.run()` on all registered runner beans. Use runners for one-time startup tasks: data migration, cache warming, health verification. Runners execute synchronously and sequentially (ordered by `@Order`). An exception in a runner stops the application.

**Phase 7 — Ready signal:** `ApplicationReadyEvent` is published. The application is fully initialized and ready to accept traffic. Kubernetes readiness probes should depend on this event — if it hasn't fired, the application isn't ready to serve requests.

```java
@EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
public void onReady(ApplicationReadyEvent event) {
    Duration startupTime = Duration.ofMillis(
        event.getTimeTaken().toMillis());
    log.info("Application ready in {}", startupTime);
}
```

The `event.getTimeTaken()` method (Spring Boot 2.6+) gives you the total startup duration measured by the framework — more accurate than manual `System.currentTimeMillis()` tracking because it covers the entire `SpringApplication.run()` call from beginning to end.
